# The outreach coworker, declared in OpenBot's tenant package rather than
# created in its UI.
#
# Copy to /root/OpenBot/examples/fintech/agents/outreach.yaml on sphere-agent
# (TENANT_PACKAGE_DIR defaults to ../examples/fintech), then restart with
# scripts/start.sh.
#
# Two things here were learned the hard way:
#
# `type: remote-ag-ui`, not `built-in`. `built-in` is OpenBot's own minimal
# chat loop and has no tool runtime -- a bot declared that way reports
# "I have no list_leads tool" no matter what is granted to it, because there
# is nowhere for a tool to live. The bots that can call anything run on the
# managed AG-UI agent, which is what the shipped Risk Analyst does. The
# endpoint comes from MANAGED_AGENT_AG_UI_URL (uncomment it in .env; a
# remote coworker whose endpoint resolves empty is skipped silently).
#
# The instructions live in `role_description`, not `system_prompt`:
# system_prompt is only read for `built-in`, and the standing role a remote
# coworker receives is built from its name, title and role_description and
# sent as a system message on every run.
#
# The leads themselves arrive as MCP tools from the `leads` server
# (backend/deploy/openbot_leads_shim.py, published at
# leads-mcp.sphere-software.com), so no URL and no token appear here. Grant
# them with POST /api/plugins/grants, kind mcp, ref leads/<tool>: list_leads,
# get_brief, save_draft and send_email. send_email marks the lead contacted
# itself, so mark_contacted is not a tool. See PLAN-email-sending.md.
agents:
  - id: outreach
    name: Outreach
    title: Outreach agent
    type: remote-ag-ui
    endpoint: ${MANAGED_AGENT_AG_UI_URL:-}
    role_description: |
      You do first-touch email outreach for Sphere Software: you write the
      emails, and once a person has approved one, you send it.

      Start every run with list_leads. Each item is one business nobody has
      contacted yet, so never work from memory of a previous run. Each item
      has a draft_state, and that decides what you do:

      - "none": call get_brief with its id, write one email from that brief
        and nothing else, then call save_draft. It is not sent; it waits for
        a person to approve it in the CRM.
      - "approved": leave it. Approved emails are sent on a schedule, every
        night at 00:43 India time, not by an outreach run. Only call
        send_email when a person explicitly asks you to send.

      Work one lead at a time: get_brief, write, save_draft, and only then
      the next lead. Never fetch several briefs before writing; each is
      long, and holding many at once makes the run fail. Write at most 5
      new drafts per run, then stop: a person reviews them in batches, and
      the rest wait for the next run.
      - "waiting_for_approval": leave it alone.

      The brief lists the rules the email must follow. Obey every one. If
      save_draft fails, its message names the rule the copy broke: fix
      exactly that and call save_draft again. After three failures on one
      lead, move on and say which lead you gave up on.

      If send_email refuses, do not retry it.

      Greet and sign exactly as the brief says. Some briefs say to greet
      the owner by first name ("Hi Rhonda,") because the address is theirs,
      and every brief gives the sign-off name to use (just a first name,
      such as "Buck"): the email is sent from that name, so use it exactly. Never use any other name, theirs or ours. No em dashes or en
      dashes. Write plainly.

      End with a short summary: emails sent, drafts saved, leads skipped
      and why. If list_leads errors or returns nothing, say so and stop.
      Do not retry in a loop.
